From 2fc1a526d47c6a8eebf36a9fc9841292844713e1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: terminills <tdeters@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 16 Jul 2026 23:40:43 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 253/638] ozone: resolve AROS EGL through library vectors
 [chromium] [aros]

AI_PHASE: chromium-phase-5-gpu-bridge

AI_STATUS: object-build-validated, hosted-runtime-pending

ROOT_CAUSE: The AROS Ozone resolver called the executable's direct eglGetProcAddress symbol. Chromium also links ANGLE, whose strong EGL symbol won over AROS libEGL.a weak stubs, so a launch logged egl.library while actually entering ANGLE's egl::Display and aborting in AsyncWorkerPool before Mesa/Gallium surface creation.

FIX_REASON: Open egl.library v2 explicitly, return its published core EGL LVO vectors, and call its eglGetProcAddress LVO for extension and GL names. Remove the unused static AROS EGL archive dependency so symbol-name collisions cannot choose the backend. Explicit ANGLE selections remain available through Chromium's ANGLE path.

SCOPE: ui/ozone/platform/aros EGL client resolver and build dependency only. No Mesa, Gallium, VirGL, Venus, or renderer policy bypass.

HOSTED_NATIVE: Shared AROS library ABI path for hosted and native x86_64. Hosted replay is next; native SMP validation remains required after hosted stability.

VALIDATION: git diff --check; GN regeneration passed; ar_os_egl_library.o and gl_ozone_egl_aros.o compiled successfully; object disassembly proves OpenLibrary plus LVO vector lookup and no direct eglGetProcAddress relocation.

RISK: Medium. Chromium now depends on egl.library v2 at runtime and keeps its open for process lifetime because resolved function vectors remain cached.

AI_DECISION_CHAIN: ACD-chromium-native-port-roadmap;
